  <bioghist id="aspace_de9b44ed336d8c3650744ce906503b66">
    <head>Historical Note</head>
<p>Formed in 1983, Artists Call Against U.S. Intervention in Central America was a mobilization of artists and other activists to raise awareness of and protest against United States military intervention in squashing left-wing governments in Central America. Founding members of the group included Lucy R. Lippard, Doug Ashford, Julie Ault, Coosje van Bruggen, Christy Rupp, Jane Teller, Jerry Kearns, Eva Cockcroft, Louise Bourgeois, Leon Golub, Daniel Flores, and Claes Oldenburg. The group organized exhibitions, readings, and other public events from 1983-1984. This culminated in January 1984, when Artists Call, the Institute for Arts and Letters of El Salvador in Exile (INALSE), and Sandinista Association of Cultural Workers (ASTC) organized a series of events and exhibitions in New York City around the anniversary of the 1932 massacre in El Salvador. The group disbanded in 1985.</p>  </bioghist>
  <bioghist id="aspace_e7784775ed9c0e943f466f6ba11c7727">
    <head>Biographical Note</head>
<p>Julie Ault (1957-) is an artist and co-founder of Group Material, an artists' collaborative active in New York City from 1979-1996. Her art often focuses on the collaboration between artists; the intersection between art and politics; and the recontextualization of cultural objects.</p>  </bioghist>
  <prefercite id="aspace_97129bc9df5ced07269b95b00d4c4101">
    <head>Preferred Citation</head>
<p>Identification of item, date; Julie Ault Papers on Artists Call; MSS 665; box number; folder number or item identifier; Fales Library &amp; Special Collections, New York University.</p>  </prefercite>
  <processinfo id="aspace_bcb5dbd2ec9d4a9be043e29780e03723">
    <head>Processing Information</head>
<p>Processing decisions prior to 2024 are unknown. In May 2024, materials were surveyed and rehoused in archival folders and boxes. In July 2026, materials related to Artists Call were separated from the Group Material Archive (MSS 215) and made into a discrete collection. Materials were arranged alphabetically and described according to local and professional standards.</p>  </processinfo>
  <relatedmaterial id="aspace_64cd0b5c56d1e791f71549eabc432c3b">
    <head>Related Materials</head>
<p>Group Material Archive (MSS 215)</p>  </relatedmaterial>
  <scopecontent id="aspace_13ec281a25d0791fb2e6f52714bb171f">
    <head>Scope and Contents</head>
<p>The Julie Ault Papers on Artists Call contains correspondence, fliers, posters, and other publicity related to the Artists Call Against U.S. Intervention in Central America. Most of the work documented in the collection focus on their exhibitions, such as Group Material's "¡LUCHAR! An Exhibition for the People of Central America" at Taller Latinoamericano and "Timeline: The Chronicle of U.S. Intervention in Latin and Central America" at MoMA PS1 and the organizing of events in New York City around the anniversary of the 1932 massacre in El Salvador. The collection also includes clippings and articles related to United States military intervention in Latin America in the 1970s and 1980s, specifically in Nicaragua and El Salvador, as well as newsletters and other promotional material from Latin American cultural and political organizations aligned with Artists Call.</p>  </scopecontent>
